As part of a GTE sponsored research project here at Harvey Mudd College, a
team of students (of which I am a part) are starting a project to enhance
the ILMI protocol (which is used for ATM networks) from a security point of 
view.  ILMI is based on SNMP, and our enhancements will be to include the
added security features of SNMPv2u into a new, backwards-compatable version
of ILMI (temporarily called ILMI+).

It is our hope that this security enhancement will become widespread among
the ATM community (especially ATM on Linux).

At this time, we are looking for anyone who has experience linking two
Linux machines via ATM without the use of a ATM Switch.  This is, 
in theory, possible (much to the supprise of many people).

Due to the constraints of the project, we cannot accept programming assistance
from outside our group.  However, any and all theoretical assistance 
(including useful experiences, documentation, and any other helpful 
information) is greatly appreciated.

To offer assistance or for more information, please contact me (Matthew Dharm)
at Matthew_Dharm@hmc.edu.  We do not regularly read newsgroups, so e-mail is
the best way to contact us.

