CLUE (Colorado Linux User Enthusiasts) Newsletter, February 1997
======================================================

CLUE is open to anyone with an interest in Linux.  Whether you are a Unix
   expert or are simply new to Linux and want to learn more about the
   Internet and Operating System phenomena that Linux represents. All 
   interested parties are invited to attend CLUE User Group meetings.

   Let's learn from each other!

The Colorado Linux Users & Enthusiasts (CLUE), normally meets the
   third Thursday of every month. The next meeting will be at
   7:00pm on February 20th, 1997. The location will be Smoky Hill High
   School, located at 16100 E. Smoky Hill Rd. in Southeast
   Aurora.  A software library is available and door prizes
   are available at many meetings.

   CLUE extends its many thanks to Smoky Hill High School and
   their faculty sponsor, Darlene Olguin, for the use of their 
   facilities.

======================================================

This Month's Topic: 
   "The Future of the Linux kernel" -- A Presentation by Linus Torvalds

   February's meeting will be a video tape presentation by Linus Torvalds,
   recorded at the UseLinux Convention in January 1997.  Linus gave this 
   presentation, which had to be moved from its orginal room to the largest 
   room at the show, to an overflow crowd.  The attendence at Linus' 
   session was better than the attendence at the Keynote session
   for the show which was given by Sun Microsystems James Gosling, 
   the man credited with developing JAVA.

   The USENIX/UseLinux show guide gave the following description of
   Linus' session:

      The Future of Linux!  The Lead developer of Linux talks about the
      future of Linux kernel development, including topics such as
      advanced memory management techniques, high speed networking,
      and portability.

   You don't want to miss this meetting!

   Meeting Schedule:
   7:00-7:10       Introduction 
   7:10-8:40       "The Future of the Linux Kernel" by Linux Torvalds
   8:40-8:55       Anouncements/Software Library Update/Door Prizes
   8:55-9:00       Conclusion 
   9:00-10:00      CLUE Board Meeting

   Please Note:  Smoky Hill High School asks that we be out of the 
   building no later than 9pm.

Next Month's Topic:
   The first of CLUE's 1997 biannual Installation Festivals is coming up 
   in April.  In order to prepare for this event, the March CLUE meeting 
   will be a General Installation Question and Answer Session.  If you've 
   had problems installing any of the various Linux distributions or if 
   you have experience installing and configuring various hardware you'll 
   want to be at this meeting.  Bring your list of installation issues!

   Details on the Installation Festival, including date/time and location,
   will be provided in next months CLUE Newsletter.

Notes from the January 1997 meeting:

   "IP Masquerading"
   By Eric Gustafson
   http://www.clue.denver.co.us/~egustafs/CLUE/masq-talk.html

   With the set of available Internet IP addresses rapidly diminishing,
   Eric presented this timely discussion regarding configuring a Linux 
   server to allow your home or business network to look like a single 
   host on the internet.  With this configuration, only the server need 
   know about how to route to hosts on your local network.  Hosts on the
   Internet only need to know how to get to the single Linux IP Masquerading
   server.

   Note that a discussion on a similar topic, IP Aliasing - allowing one
   host to answer to multiple IP address, is available in the March issue 
   of Linux Journal in an article titled "A Guide to Virtual Services".

