			 The LUUG Lectures

		  UKUUG	- London Unix User Group


    Thursday 27th February 1997, 19:00

    Room 639, Level 6, Institute of Education,
    20 Bedford Way, London WC1



    Linux and O/S Research

    or How Linux Saved My Network


    Matt Welsh
    University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory

    Author of Running Linux



    The use of Linux as a key tool in advanced operating systems
    research has proven to be invaluable in a number of recent
    projects. This talk will describe my research at Cornell University
    on the U-Net project, which allows user applications to directly
    access network interfaces for low-latency and high-bandwidth
    communication over ATM and Fast Ethernet.  Our ability to freely
    enhance,  instrument, and even circumvent the Linux kernel enabled
    this research to explore the design space of such network
    interfaces using modern PC hardware and without the hindrance of
    source-code licenses. I'll describe some of the cunning hacks used
    in the U-Net system and talk about the lessons we've learned using
    Linux in this context.

    This is an informal talk meant to turn people on to Linux hacking
    and O/S research --- no previous experience necessary!

    Matt Welsh is currently a research assistant with the University of
    Cambridge Computer Laboratory and is author of `Running Linux',
    published by O'Reilly and Associates.


    Admission is free, and is open to everyone.

    Special Offer

    Running Linux and other O'Reilly books  will  be  available
    during the event at 25% discount



    Informal discussions will continue after the lecture at the Rising
    Sun in Tottenham Court Road before moving on to eat, probably at
    the Cypriana.  (The nearest Underground station here is Goodge
    Street)



    The lectures are sponsored by the UK Unix systems User Group.

    For further information on LUUG Lectures, contact Andrew Findlay.
    Phone: 01895 203066 E-mail: Andrew.Findlay@brunel.ac.uk


