I have uploaded a new version of SIDPLAY/Linux (v1.23.1) to sunsite.unc.edu.
The package is named sidplay-1.23.1-src.tar.gz and is currently located in
/Incoming. It is expected to show up in /pub/Linux/apps/sound/players/.

Basically, SIDPLAY is just an ordinary music player. More specifically,
SIDPLAY emulates the sound interface device chip (MOS 6581, known as SID)
and the micro processor unit (MOS 6510) of the Commodore 64 on your computer.
Therefore it is able to load and execute C64 machine code programs which
produce music or sound. In general these are independent fragments of code
and data which were ripped from games and demonstration programs and were
transfered directly from the C64.

The idea for SIDPLAY is based on the Amiga program ``The 100 most remembered
C64 game-tunes'' and its follower PlaySID} by Per Haakan Sundell and Ron Birk.

For more information check out the SIDPLAY home page:

  http://www.geocities.com/SiliconValley/Lakes/5147

Around 5000 different sidtunes from Commodore 64 games and demos are available
on the Internet. A lot even contain several sub-songs.

