                          Sqsh Release 1.5
                             18-Feb-1997

The following is available from: 

              http://www.voicenet.com/~gray/sqsh.html

Announcing sqsh-1.5, the ever popular replacement for Sybase's
'isql' program available on most UNIX platforms.  This version
provides several feature enhancements over the 1.4 release, as
well as a complete re-write of the database internals to use
Sybase CT-Library rather than DB-Library, opening up the available
platforms to support Linux (and potentially NetBSD, FreeBSD, etc.)

What is SQSH?
- -=-=-=-=-=-=-

   Sqsh (pronounced skwish) is short for SQshelL (pronounced s-q-shell),
   it is intended as a replacement for the venerable 'isql' program
   supplied by Sybase.  It came about due to years of frustration of
   trying to do real work with a program that was never meant to
   perform real work.

   Sqsh is much more than a nice prompt (a la 'dsql', from David B.
   Joyner), it is intended to provide much of the functionality provided
   by a good shell, such as variables, redirection, pipes, back-grounding,
   job control, history, command completion, and dynamic configuration.
   Also, as a by-product of the design, it is remarkably easy to extend
   and add functionality.

   The following are a brief list of the more notable features of sqsh
   (a '*' indicates a new or modified feature of 1.5):

      o  Command line editing (using GNU Readline library)
      o  Variables
      o  Redirection ("go > file") and piping ("go | more")
      o  Command substitution ("SELECT * FROM `echo syscolumns`")
      o  Background queries and job control ("go &")
      o  Command aliasing
      o  SQL batch history
      o  Configurable script aborting and exit status
      o  BCP result sets between servers
      o  Direct remote procedure calls
      *  Multiple display styles (horizontal, vertical, bcp, and HTML)
      *  X Windows support
      o  Session locking
      *  Smart semicolon go ("sp_who;")
      o  Configurable prompt
      o  Dynamic reconnection and server switching
      o  Configurable keyword completion (using GNU Readline library)
      o  Named SQL buffers
      o  Specify database context on command line

Platforms
- -=-=-=-=-

   Sqsh was designed with portability in mind and has been successfully
   compiled on most major UNIX platforms supported by Sybase, such as
   Linux, HP-UX, AIX, IRIX, SunOS, Solaris, Dynix, OSF/1, DEC Unix, SCO, 
   NeXT, and CP/M (just kidding).  

   And, no, it has not been ported to NT.

Changes Since 1.4
- -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

   The following is a condensed list of the more noticable changes
   since 1.4, a more detailed list may be found from the Sqsh Home
   Page and ChangeLog:

      o Ported to CT-Lib - if you have built sqsh in the past, make
        sure you re-read the provided README and INSTALL for build
        differences.
      o Enhanced 'configure' to be a smarter.
      o Replaced entire signal handling mechanism. Will now use 
        POSIX signal handling when available (SGI users, make
        sure you read README.SGI concerning this change).
      o Added HTML display style to output HTML <TABLE> constructs.
      o Added X windows support for displaying result set in a separate
        window (requires X11R4 or later).
      o Added $batch_pause environment variable to cause a "hit enter
        to continue" message following each batch executed.
      o Semicolon will no longer be interpreted within comments, both
        "/* */" style and "--" style.
      o Added '-e <statement>' to \loop command to execute contents
        of <statement> within its own SQL Buffer (thanks to Dan
        Roitman for the patch).
      o Added '-r sqshrc' option to allow you override the name of the
        .sqshrc file to read upon start-up.  If sqshrc is not supplied
        then no startup file is read.
      o Added -B flag so that sqsh will now attempt to turn off
        buffering of stdin, stdout, and stderr upon start-up.
        This should allow it to be controlled by such libraries
        as 'expect'.
      o '\go' without an empty command buffer will re-run last command
        (thanks again to Dan).
      o $thresh_exit will only be applicable while in a non-interactive
        session.
      o Added FAQ to the distribution.

Mailing List On-Line
- -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

   Thanks to the tireless efforts of Pablo Sanchez (pablo@sgi.com,
   our fearless maintainer of the Sybase FAQ), and the excellent
   people at SGI (http://www.sgi.com), the sqsh mailing list is
   now available for use!  Keep track of the latest developments.
   Offer suggestions for future additions. Offer me money  :)

   To subscribe, send mail to

      external-majordomo@postofc.corp.sgi.com

   With a *body* of:

      subscribe sqsh-users [optional e-mail address]

   Or with a body of just:

      help

   for a list of commands.


Availability
- -=-=-=-=-=-=-

   Sqsh may be found on the following sites:

     http://www.voicenet.com/~gray/sqsh.html
     ftp://poseidon.csci.unt.edu/pub/sqsh
     ftp://ftp.netcom.com/pub/he/heyjude/gray
     ftp://davox2.davox.com/pub/sqsh

Licensing
- -=-=-=-=-=-

   99% of the software that I use is free, therefore I like to give back
   in kind. Sqsh is held under the GNU General Public License (GPL) and
   therefore may be freely distributed under the terms of this license.

If you have any questions, comments, etc, or you want to go mountain
biking, offer me hardware, money, or buy me a beer :), send me e-mail
at gray@voicenet.com.

Enjoy!
- -scott
